$ABCD$ is a quadrilateral whose vertices do not all lie on one circle.
$\angle DAB = 92^{\circ}$ and $\angle BCD = 97^{\circ}$.
Is $\angle DAB + \angle BCD$ guaranteed to equal $180^{\circ}$?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes}
\coordinate (A) at (-1.533,0.033);
\coordinate (B) at (-0.086,1.586);
\coordinate (C) at (1.467,0.033);
\coordinate (D) at (0.151,-1.651);
\draw (A) -- (B);
\draw (B) -- (C);
\draw (C) -- (D);
\draw (D) -- (A);
\node[above left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[above right] at (B) {$B$};
\node[below right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[below left] at (D) {$D$};
\pic[draw, "$92^{\circ}$", angle radius=0.4cm, angle eccentricity=2] {angle = D--A--B};
\pic[draw, "$88^{\circ}$", angle radius=0.4cm, angle eccentricity=2] {angle = A--B--C};
\pic[draw, "$97^{\circ}$", angle radius=0.4cm, angle eccentricity=2] {angle = B--C--D};
\pic[draw, "$83^{\circ}$", angle radius=0.4cm, angle eccentricity=2] {angle = C--D--A};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

